From f66c8fa8f0a9cd626732a921e1bf47c93cf2e6ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:27:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] AArch64 linker: DWARF, a complete section table and
 .symtab

The AArch64 linker copied no .debug_* section into any output. Static
and dynamic executables had no section-header table (e_shnum 0) and
shared objects listed only their 7-8 dynamic sections, so gdb had no
file:line and readelf, nm and perf had nothing to read.

- Debug sections merge after every allocated section (the x86-64
  merge_debug_sections_elf64), honouring -s/-S, and follow the load
  image in the file at address 0.
- Their relocations resolve statically and in place (arm/linker/
  debug.rs), never through the GOT/PLT or as dynamic relocations:
  ABS64, ABS32 (bitfield overflow), PREL64 and PREL32 (signed), the
  set GCC 14 and lccc emit. References into COMDAT losers or
  --gc-sections victims take bfd's tombstone, 1 in .debug_ranges and
  .debug_loc, else 0. The generic relocation loops skip unloaded
  sections.
- linker_common/shdr.rs writes .symtab, .strtab, .shstrtab and the
  header table from a list of section descriptors whose sh_link and
  symbol sections are names, not indices. All three writers use it;
  the shared writer's hand-counted table is gone. .symtab holds local
  functions and objects and every defined non-imported global (the
  x86-64 policy); mapping symbols stay out.

Section headers for t.c: static 0 -> 29, dynamic 0 -> 30; shared
object 8 -> 27 (lccc objects) and 31 (GCC objects). readelf -a -w
reports no warning on any of them. DW_AT_low_pc, the line table and
.symtab agree on every function. Linking aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc 14
-g -O0 and -O2 objects (loclists, rnglists, .debug_str, PREL32 in
.debug_line), a gdb-multiarch session on qemu-aarch64 (break helper,
bt, info args, print a static array, finish) reads identically to
the same objects linked by GNU ld 2.44, apart from the pid.

New linker case crossarch_debug_sections covers a static and a dynamic
executable, a shared object, -S, -s and the GCC -O2 objects. Linker
suite: 322 pass, 0 fail.
