From c89316258e1e82a79a25c486796c45a5f3761b89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:32:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] riscv linker: DWARF, section tables and TLS across DSOs

Debug info (the RISC-V linker dropped every .debug_* section, and
wrote an incomplete section table, so gdb saw no symbols):
- debug.rs merges and relocates DWARF statically through the
  shared linker_common placement (R_RISCV_32/64, ADD/SUB 8..64,
  SET 6..32, SUB6, 32_PCREL, SET/SUB_ULEB128), appended after the
  image; debug
  sections are never dynamically relocated.
- Executables and shared objects get bfd's complete section table
  and order, a .symtab with local symbols, and .dynsym st_shndx
  values taken from the final, ordered headers.
- -Wl,-S drops DWARF, -s also drops .symtab.
Against GNU ld 2.44 on rv64 -g/-gno-column/-O2 -g links: DWARF is
identical modulo placement, gdb shows the same lines and variables,
and readelf -aW -w reports nothing.

TLS (got.rs replaces the one-word-per-name GOT):
- GotLayout allocates one entry per (symbol, access kind): an
  address word, an initial-exec TP offset, or a two-word tls_index
  for general-dynamic; a name accessed both ways no longer aliases
  one slot.
- Shared objects: a preemptible symbol gets R_RISCV_64,
  TLS_TPREL64 or DTPMOD64 + DTPREL64 by name; otherwise RELATIVE,
  TPREL64 against the object with the template offset, or
  DTPMOD64 plus a static DTPREL (offset - 0x800), as in bfd.
  Previously these were wrong or refused.
- Executables relax GD to LE for their own variables, and import
  a DSO's variable through loader-filled TPREL64 or
  DTPMOD64/DTPREL64 slots.

Tests (tests/linker, both fail on the parent of the AArch64 commit):
- crossarch_debug_sections also links RISC-V: a warning-free
  section table and readelf -w, DWARF and .symtab present.
- tls_shared_matrix: TLS variables exported from a .so and used by
  an executable, plus static links, in the default, -fPIC (and
  TLSDESC/traditional on AArch64) and initial-exec models, on
  x86-64, i686, AArch64 and RISC-V (cross arches run under qemu and
  skip without <triple>-gcc and qemu-<arch>). 172 links, ~15 s.
